<PicassoImporterCase
  lang="en"
  title="Picasso Importer on TrueNAS"
  description="An end-to-end CBCT workflow from Windows to Picasso Viewer using personal SMB inboxes, ZFS snapshots, and no-overwrite publication."
>
  <p>
    The pilot tested how Picasso Viewer and Ez3D2009 open cone-beam CT (CBCT) studies from a shared,
    read-only directory. The next task was to add new studies to the same archive. We tested the first
    import manually from Windows, then moved scheduled intake to TrueNAS. The viewing pilot is documented in
    <a href="https://notes.shandrov.ru/notes/storage-backup/truenas-picasso-ez3d2009-pilot-part-1/">Part 1 of the series</a>.
    Part 3 covers the <a href="https://notes.shandrov.ru/notes/storage-backup/truenas-picasso-importer-production-part-3/">workflow diagram and end-to-end acceptance</a>.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Two SMB shares with different permissions</h2>
    <p>
      The uploader sends each export to the Inbox as a separate package. Once the transfer completes,
      it checks the copy with SHA-256, writes a JSON readiness marker, and renames the temporary directory
      to its final name. Queue readiness and publication to the archive are recorded separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Python importer runs locally on the NAS. Picasso Viewer reads published studies from a separate,
      read-only SMB share. Workstation write access is limited to the Inbox, and the importer writes to the
      archive. On TrueNAS, cron checks the queue every five minutes. An operator can also process the next
      job or a specific job manually for troubleshooting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>From a ready package to the viewer directory</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Capture the input.</strong> Once it finds the marker, the importer reads the package
        from a ZFS snapshot. The server checks the file count and byte count against the marker, then
        compares its own manifests of the live folder and snapshot using file names, sizes and SHA-256
        hashes. The client hash in the marker is useful for troubleshooting the transfer.
        <a href="https://openzfs.github.io/openzfs-docs/Basic%20Concepts/Datasets/Snapshots%20and%20Clones.html">OpenZFS documentation</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Validate the structure and look for duplicates.</strong> A server-side package contains
        a flat set of <code>.dcm</code> files and the original <code>Default.npj</code> from the same export.
        The importer verifies the project size and SHA-256, reads DICOM headers and scans the archive snapshot
        for overlapping Study Instance UIDs and SOP Instance UIDs. A full UID match for a new submission
        returns <code>DUPLICATE_STUDY_SKIPPED</code>. The <code>ALREADY_IMPORTED_EXACT</code> status
        applies to the same previously published job when the input, archive and post-import snapshot
        manifests match. Re-exports with new UIDs can require operator review.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stage and publish.</strong> The importer assembles the package outside the directory that
        Viewer reads and checks the SHA-256 hashes of the staged files after copying. The final operation
        uses <code>renameat2</code> with <code>RENAME_NOREPLACE</code> to move the directory within the same
        mounted filesystem. The complete directory appears at once, and an existing directory with the
        same name is protected from replacement.
        <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/rename.2.html">Linux rename(2) manual</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The original upload stays in the Inbox. If a job fails, the available verification records,
      snapshots and temporary files are retained for investigation. For a failure around the final rename,
      check staging, the destination and the job result before retrying. The exit status alone cannot
      tell you whether the directory had already appeared in the archive.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the tests showed</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Windows pilot.</strong> All 537 files in the earlier manual import matched their SHA-256
        hashes (536 DICOM files and one NPJ project). The study opened in Picasso Viewer and Ez3D2009 on
        one workstation.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Server-side importer.</strong> The first canary run on TrueNAS and its repeat both returned
        <code>ALREADY_IMPORTED_EXACT</code>. The archive was unchanged and the upload remained in the Inbox.
        The schedule was enabled following the canary runs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>End-to-end acceptance.</strong> Two new complete exports were submitted from PowerShell and
        from a desktop shortcut. Both jobs reached <code>IMPORTED_VERIFIED</code> and opened from Production in Viewer.
        A separate workstation verified automatic shortcut setup at Windows sign-in.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</PicassoImporterCase>
